IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wills-dates`
IMPORT `wills-rules`

-- Wills Act 2000 (NT) -- the questions of the WILL-MAKER.
--
-- SEAT: the individual making, changing, revoking or depositing a will in the Northern Territory (the testator), including
-- a minor who wants to make one and a person whose will was made for them by Court order. "What can I leave by my will?",
-- "Can I make a valid will?", "Is my will properly signed and witnessed?", "Does my marriage revoke my will?", "What does my
-- divorce do to my will?", "Is my change to my will effective?", "Have I revoked my will?", "Can I get my deposited will
-- back?". The Court, the Registrar and the prescribed person (the Public Trustee) get no seat: their satisfactions, orders
-- and waivers are asked as facts. The family's questions after a death are in wills-seat-family.l4.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of wills-rules.l4.
-- No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL COPY: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word reprint REPW007 of the Wills Act
-- 2000 (NT), as in force at 1 January 2022, not an official version. Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned
-- by the Northern Territory. Quoted under the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October
-- 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 2000 (NT): the will-maker's questions`

§§ `What I can leave, and who may make a will`

@export Can I leave this property by my will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 6)? Yes for property you are entitled to at your death, and for property your personal representative becomes entitled to as such after your death; no for property you hold as trustee at your death.
GIVEN `hold` IS A `My hold on the property at my death` @desc How you will hold the property at your death: entitled to it yourself; your personal representative becomes entitled to it, as personal representative, after your death; you hold it as trustee for someone else; or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I leave this property by my will` MEANS
    `s 6 -- I may leave this property by my will` `hold`

@export Is the will I am making valid as far as my age goes (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 7, 18)? A will made by someone under 18 is not valid unless they are married, make it in contemplation of a marriage that then takes place, or the Supreme Court authorises it. Being of sound mind (testamentary capacity) is a separate, common law test not answered here.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years on the day you make the will
      `status` IS A `Marital status` @desc Your marital status when you make the will: never married; married now; or married before but not now (divorced or widowed)
      `contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are making the will in contemplation of a marriage you are planning
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under Part 3 of the Act authorising you to make this will in these terms
GIVETH A `Whether my will is valid as to my age`
`Is my will valid as to my age` MEANS
    `ss 7, 18 -- my will is valid as to my age` `age` `status` `contemplation` `court order`

@export Can I revoke my will although I am under 18 (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 7(2), 18)? Yes if you are married, if the will was made in contemplation of marriage, if you have been married and the will was made while you were married, or with a Supreme Court order. Otherwise the Act does not say.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years now
      `status` IS A `Marital status` @desc Your marital status now: never married; married now; or married before but not now (divorced or widowed)
      `made in contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will you want to revoke was made in contemplation of a marriage
      `made while married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will you want to revoke was made while you were married
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has made an order under Part 3 authorising you to revoke it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I revoke my will as a minor` MEANS
    `ss 7, 18 -- I may revoke my will although a minor` `age` `status` `made in contemplation` `made while married` `court order`

@export I am under 18: can the Supreme Court authorise me to make, change or revoke a will in terms it approves (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 18(1), (2))? You or someone on your behalf may apply; the Court must first be satisfied of three things, and may then make the order on conditions.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years now
      `understand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied you understand the nature and effect of the proposed will, change or revocation and the extent of the property it disposes of
      `reflects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the proposal accurately reflects your intentions
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied it is reasonable in all the circumstances to make the order
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court authorise my will as a minor` MEANS
    `s 18(2) -- the Court may authorise my will as a minor` `age` `understand` `reflects` `reasonable`

@export I am under 18 and the Supreme Court authorised my will: is the will valid (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 18(4))? Only if it is signed and witnessed as the Act requires, the Registrar is one of the witnesses, and it follows the conditions of the order. The Registrar then keeps it or deposits it.
GIVEN `executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was signed and witnessed as the Act requires (in writing, signed by you before two witnesses present together, who then signed in your presence)
      `registrar witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar of the Supreme Court was one of the witnesses who signed
      `conditions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in accordance with the conditions of the Court's order
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my Court-authorised will as a minor valid` MEANS
    `s 18(4) -- my Court-authorised will as a minor is valid` `executed` `registrar witness` `conditions`

§§ `Signing and witnessing my will`

@export Is my will validly signed and witnessed (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 8, 9, 11)? It must be in writing, signed by you (or by someone in your presence at your direction) intending it as your will, with two or more witnesses present together who can see you sign, and at least two of them must sign in your presence. A witness need not know it is a will; the signature need not be at the foot; no attestation clause is needed.
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in writing (on paper or other material)
      `who signed` IS A `Who signed the will` @desc Who signed the will: you; someone else in your presence and at your direction; someone else not in your presence or not at your direction; or nobody
      `intending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the signature was made with the intention of executing the document as your will
      `witnesses present` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses, each able to see you sign, were present at the same time when you signed or acknowledged your signature
      `witnesses signed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those witnesses then attested and signed the will in your presence (they need not sign in each other's presence)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will validly signed and witnessed` MEANS
    `ss 8, 11 -- my will is validly executed` `in writing` `who signed` `intending` `witnesses present` `witnesses signed`

@export I made my will under another place's law: is it properly executed (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 46)? Yes if it was executed as the law of the place where it was signed requires, or the law of your domicile, habitual residence or nationality when you made it or at your death (or, on a ship or aircraft, the law of the place it is most closely connected with). If none fits, the Territory's own rules may still be met.
GIVEN `place made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was executed as the law of the place where it was signed requires
      `domicile` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was executed as the law of the place of your domicile or habitual residence requires, either when you made it or at your death
      `nationality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was executed as the law of a country of which you were a national requires, either when you made it or at your death
      `vessel` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was made on a ship or aircraft and executed as the law of the place most closely connected with that ship or aircraft requires
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will made under another law properly executed` MEANS
    `s 46 -- my will is properly executed under another place's law` `place made` `domicile` `nationality` `vessel`

@export Is my will valid in form as an international will under the Washington Convention (Wills Act 2000 (NT) Part 5A and the Annex, Arts 1-5)? It must be one person's will, in writing, declared as your will before two witnesses and an authorised person (an Australian lawyer or public notary), signed or acknowledged by you before them, and signed by them in your presence. If it fails as an international will it may still be valid as an ordinary will.
GIVEN `one person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is your will alone (not a joint will of two or more people in one document)
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in writing, in any language, by hand or any other means
      `authorised` IS A `The authorised person` @desc Who acted as the authorised person: an Australian legal practitioner in Australia; a public notary of an Australian jurisdiction in Australia; a person authorised under the law of another country party to the Convention; or nobody authorised
      `witnesses` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses were present with the authorised person
      `declared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you declared before the witnesses and the authorised person that the document is your will and you know its contents
      `signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed the will, or acknowledged your earlier signature, in their presence
      `attested` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the witnesses and the authorised person then and there signed the will in your presence
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will valid as an international will` MEANS
    `Part 5A, Annex Arts 1-5 -- my will is valid in form as an international will` `one person` `in writing` `authorised` `witnesses` `declared` `signed` `attested`

§§ `Changing, revoking and reviving my will`

@export Has my will been revoked by what was done to it (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 13)? Only by a later will, a signed and witnessed writing revoking it, destroying it (by you, or by someone in your presence at your direction) intending to revoke it, writing on it so that the Court is satisfied you meant to revoke it, a Court order for a minor or a person without capacity, or by marriage or divorce (asked separately).
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was done to the will` @desc What was done to the will: a later will that revokes it; a signed and witnessed writing revoking it; you destroyed it intending to revoke it; someone else destroyed it in your presence at your direction; someone else destroyed it not in your presence or not at your direction; destroyed by accident or without intention; written on or dealt with so that the Court is satisfied you meant to revoke it; a Court order; an unwitnessed statement; or a later will that does not revoke it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my will revoked` MEANS
    `s 13 -- my will is revoked by what was done` `what`

@export What does my marriage do to my will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 14)? Marriage revokes a will, except gifts to and appointments of the person you are married to when you die, unless the will was made in contemplation of that marriage, or is expressed to be made in contemplation of marriage generally. Entering a de facto relationship does not revoke a will.
GIVEN `married since` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have married since you made the will
      `this marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the will in contemplation of that marriage (whether or not the will says so)
      `marriage generally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will says it is made in contemplation of marriage generally
GIVETH A `What my marriage does to my will`
`What does my marriage do to my will` MEANS
    `s 14 -- what my marriage does to my will` `married since` `this marriage` `marriage generally`

@export Does my divorce or annulment revoke this part of my will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 15)? It revokes gifts to your former spouse, their appointment as executor, trustee, advisory trustee or guardian, and powers of appointment by or for them, as if they had died before you; but not their appointment as trustee for property left for beneficiaries who include their children, nor a power they may exercise only for your joint children, and not if the will shows a contrary intention. Separation alone revokes nothing.
GIVEN `how ended` IS A `How the marriage ended` @desc How the marriage ended: a divorce order took effect under the Family Law Act 1975; a decree of nullity by the Family Court; dissolved or annulled overseas and recognised in Australia; dissolved or annulled overseas and not recognised; or separated without divorce
      `part` IS A `Part of my will about my former spouse` @desc Which part of the will you are asking about: a gift to your former spouse; their appointment as executor, guardian or advisory trustee; their appointment as trustee for beneficiaries who include their children; their appointment as trustee of other property; a power they may exercise only in favour of your joint children; another power of appointment by or for them; or a part that does not concern them
      `contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows, or it is otherwise established, that you meant this part to stand despite a divorce
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my divorce revoke this part of my will` MEANS
    `s 15 -- my divorce revokes this part of my will` `how ended` `part` `contrary intention`

@export Is the change I made to my will effective (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 16)? A change made after the will was signed counts only if it is signed and witnessed like a will (the signatures may be in the margin or on a note on the will), was made under a Court order, or the Court accepts it after your death. Initials alone are not enough.
GIVEN `after execution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the change was made after the will was signed and witnessed
      `how` IS A `How the change was made` @desc How the change was made: signed and witnessed like a will; made as a minor under a Court order; made for you under a Court order while you lacked capacity; accepted by the Court after your death; words obliterated so they can no longer be read; or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my change to my will effective` MEANS
    `s 16 -- my change to my will is effective` `after execution` `how`

@export Is my revoked will revived (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 17)? A revoked will (or part) is revived by signing and witnessing it again, or by a new will showing an intention to revive it; it then counts as made on the day it is revived.
GIVEN `how` IS A `How I tried to revive it` @desc How you tried to revive the revoked will: re-executed it; executed a new will showing an intention to revive it; or something else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my revoked will revived` MEANS
    `s 17 -- my revoked will is revived` `how`

§§ `Depositing my will for safekeeping`

@export Can my will be deposited for safekeeping in this envelope (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 50(2))? Only in a sealed envelope marked with your name and address, the name and address of each executor (as in the will), the date of the will and the name of the person depositing it. Wills are deposited with the prescribed person, or the Public Trustee if none is prescribed.
GIVEN `envelope` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in a sealed envelope marked with your name and address, each executor's name and address, the date of the will and the depositor's name
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my will be deposited in this envelope` MEANS
    `s 50(2) -- my will may be deposited in this envelope` `envelope`

@export Must I pay the fee to deposit my will (Wills Act 2000 (NT) s 50(4))? Yes, the prescribed fee, unless the will is a Court-authorised will, it is deposited because your lawyer has died or stopped practising in the Territory, or the fee is waived. The amount is set by regulations not included here.
GIVEN `why` IS A `Why the will is being deposited` @desc Why the will is being deposited: you are depositing your own will; it is a Court-authorised will; or your lawyer has died or has stopped (or is about to stop) practising law in the Territory
      `waived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the prescribed person has waived the fee
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I pay the will deposit fee` MEANS
    `s 50(4) -- I must pay the deposit fee` `why` `waived`

@export Can I get my deposited will back (Wills Act 2000 (NT) ss 51(1), (2), 25(2))? On a written application, the prescribed person must give it to you (or to someone you authorise in writing), unless you are under 18 or lack testamentary capacity. A will made for you by Court order while you lacked capacity can be withdrawn only if you regain capacity or the Court authorises its revocation.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years now
      `capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have testamentary capacity now (you understand what a will is, what you own and who might expect to benefit)
      `statutory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made for you by Court order while you lacked testamentary capacity
      `revocation order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order authorising the revocation of that will
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied in writing to the prescribed person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get my deposited will back` MEANS
    `ss 25(2), 51(1), (2) -- I can get my deposited will back` `age` `capacity` `statutory` `revocation order` `in writing`
